    {
      "id": 116,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        -697.3665771484375,
        1093.5635986328125
      ],
      "size": [
        497.0442199707031,
        470.9059753417969
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 17,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {
        "widget_ue_connectable": {}
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "This is V2V, using a reference image for styling (first frame), and a video for the structure and action. \n\nUnify width and height, and drive it from the constants here, not the video image resizing node that comes with the original workflow. \n\nI left remove background in, but I didn't use it.\n\n1. load the style refence image I want to end up with,\n\n2. load the video I will use as a controlnet to drive the scene and the action.\n\n3. pick the controlnet that best suits the need. You will need to plug it in to WanVaceToVideo control video slot and I also plug it into a previewer.\n\nchoose sampler settings but I didnt muck about with it much once I got it working.\n\nNOTE:\n\nI had problems when I was trying ro restyle a person if they were not in almost exactly the same position in the reference image, as they were starting out in the video. This makes sense. As such I sometimes used Shotcut or Krita to match the first frame of the video positionally (close ish was enough) similar between image and video. Otherwise works great.\n\nI also used this for bad video quality then ran the first frame through the daemon detailer workflow for improved detail, then used that as the image styler to force higher detail onto a poor video. I would have done this more but I ran out of time. There is an example of it in use in the workflow page for Footprints In Eternity on my website MarkDKBerry.com"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
